 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Things are getting tense, and Max has to organise a small team to rescue the dinner. He reassures them, and he sets them to work. Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ладно, мы разберёмся с этим.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Всё будет хорошо.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "Что нам делать сейчас?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Поехали.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Куда мы едем?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Мы никуда не едем.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Это значит, что мы начинаем.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "Теперь я всё понял.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Тогда давайте работать.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Да, за работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "У нас мало времени.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Мы должны успеть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Конечно, мы успеем.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "Тогда начинаем прямо сейчас.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Their plan, slowly.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ладно, мы разберёмся с этим.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Alright, we will figure this out.' Let us consider this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "разберёмся",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the 'we will' form to say 'sort out'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ладно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the shrugging 'okay then'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Всё будет хорошо.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Everything will be fine.' This uses ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "хорошо",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " rather than the word for 'good'. It is one of the most useful sentences in Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "Что нам делать сейчас?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What should we do now?' Now let's analyze the phrase: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "нам",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " names the people who must act, and the verb stays in its basic form.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Поехали.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Let's go.' We will focus on the sentence: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Поехали",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " comes from the word for going by vehicle. The first man in space said it as his rocket lifted off, and now it means 'let's get going' with anything.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Куда мы едем?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Where are we going?' Let's inspect the sentence: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Куда",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'where to'. If you're asking where something is, you use a different word; if you're asking where someone is heading, it's this.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Мы никуда не едем.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We are not going anywhere.' Let's examine the speech: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "никуда",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "не",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is a double negative. Russian needs both negatives here.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Это значит, что мы начинаем.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'It means that we are starting.' We will analyze the words: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "что",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " can be used to ask 'what', but it also works as 'that', joining two halves of a sentence.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "Теперь я всё понял.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Now I understand everything.' We will look at the phrase: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "понял",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is past, meaning 'I understood', but people use it for 'I get it' now. The ending fits Ilya, a man.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Тогда давайте работать.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Then let's work.' We will look at the speech: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "давайте",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " and the action in its basic form make a group suggestion. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Тогда",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " ties it to what Max just said.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Да, за работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, back to work.' Let's focus on the phrase: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "за",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "работу",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the rallying cry from the last scene.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "У нас мало времени.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We have little time.' After ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "мало",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning 'little', the word for time takes an ending that means 'of' and becomes ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "времени",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Мы должны успеть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We must be in time.' Let us examine this line: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "успеть",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'to manage to do something in time', a single word with no neat English match. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "должны",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the plural form for 'we'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Конечно, мы успеем.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Of course we will be in time.' Now we can analyze this line: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "успеем",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the 'we' form for making it in time. There is a comma after ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Конечно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", so it opens the answer like 'of course, yes'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "Тогда начинаем прямо сейчас.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Then we start right now.' We can examine the speech: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "начинаем",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'we start', and said to a group it works like 'let's start'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "прямо",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "ilya",
   "t": "сейчас",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
